# Manuscript PDF page 96

                       HERALDS OF THE CEMÍ



   I said it out loud before I’d decided to. “Abertura. Aperture.”
   “Abertura,” said my grandmother, giving it the long Spanish r.
“Opening. Lo único que cambió fue la pronunciación.”
   The only thing that changed was how they say it.
   “Yield of what?” I said. “The sugar went up twelve percent. What’s
the rest of that column?”
   She put her finger on the word Rendimiento, then on the words be‐
side the names. Oye al agua. No se calla.
   “They didn’t invent the cane,” she said. “Or the whip. Men did that,
and other men let them. Abertura came to collect.” She tapped the line
about the bell. “You work a person until he’s angry, and you ring a bell
where he would have sung, and the anger has nowhere to go. It comes
out of him. Se le sale el ánimo.” She said the word as if it belonged to
someone in the next room. “What gets a person up in the morning.
What answers back. When it spills, they catch it, and they write it
down.”
   “Write it down how?”
   She ran her finger along the column. Name, age, a number. “Lo que
se cuenta, se lleva.” What gets counted gets carried off. “A man who is
only a number has nothing left to answer with. He cuts cane and he
doesn’t fight. Más doce por ciento.”
   “And what they carry off. Where does it go?”
   “Into the next bell.” She looked out the window at the speaker over
the colmado. “Every one rings a little heavier than the last.”
   “And what’s remembered?”
   Her mouth softened. “That, they can’t carry. Lo que se recuerda,
vuelve.”
   What’s remembered comes back.



For a while I just sat there. A moped went by outside, then a vendor
singing the price of avocados. Abuela folded her hands in her lap and
watched me, as she had at dinner, and I understood she’d done this
once already, thirty years ago, alone, with a dictionary.




